MAME ctlsock: let a cursor window address INSIDE a save item of any element
size, not only a byte array.

WHY

mame-ctlsock-ram-cursor.patch gave MAME_CTL_CURSOR_ITEMS a RAM-window form,
`suffix@offset[:width]`, so a machine whose cursor position is not its own
save item can still feed the closed loop. It restricted the window to a
BYTE-sized item, because the one machine it was authored for (apple2gs) reads
its cursor out of `m_megaii_ram`, a u8 array.

riscos3 (Acorn Archimedes 310, MAME driver aa310) has a real HARDWARE cursor
and a real save item for it -- but the register is an ELEMENT of a wider
array. src/devices/machine/acorn_vidc.cpp registers

    save_pointer(NAME(m_crtc_regs), CRTC_VCER+1);      // u32[16]

and draws the pointer sprite at `m_crtc_regs[CRTC_HCSR] - m_crtc_regs[CRTC_HBSR]`,
`m_crtc_regs[CRTC_VCSR] - m_crtc_regs[CRTC_VBSR]` (acorn_vidc.cpp:516-523).
CRTC_HCSR is index 6 and CRTC_VCSR index 14 of that array, i.e. BYTE offsets
24 and 56, width 4. `find_item()` resolves `m_crtc_regs` fine, but the
historical `read()` reads element 0 (the horizontal cycle register) and the
ram-cursor window refused the item outright for having size=4 -- so the one
machine in the fleet with a readable hardware cursor register could not read
it, and MOVEA would have degraded to open-loop dead reckoning on a guest
whose quadrature keyboard discards movement magnitude (archimedes_keyb.cpp's
`update_mouse` advances the phase by ONE step per 4 kHz tick and then assigns
`m_mouse_x = x`, dropping the rest). Open loop is not an option here.

WHAT IT CHANGES

Two bounds checks, both in the ram-cursor window path:

  * `read_le()` no longer requires `size == 1`; it bounds the window against
    the item's BYTE CAPACITY, `size * count`.
  * `find_item_spec()`'s matching guard does the same.

A byte-sized item has capacity == count, so apple2gs and every other existing
caller behave BYTE FOR BYTE as before. Nothing outside the window path is
touched, no save items are added or removed, and the savestate signature is
unchanged -- the ctlsock module's covenant 1 is preserved.

ENDIANNESS. The window assembles little-endian bytes, and MAME stores save
items in host order, so a width-`size` window at a `size`-aligned offset reads
exactly that array element on a little-endian host. Every host this fleet
ships on is x86-64. A big-endian host would need the byte order flipped; that
is out of scope and would be a new knob, not a silent behaviour change.

MEASURED ON RISCOS3 (2026-09-20, lab box, aa310 + RISC OS 3.11, headless)

Before: `ctlsock: cursor window m_crtc_regs@24:4 unusable (item size=4
count=16, off=24 w=4)` and `movea=0`. After: the window resolves, `movea=1`,
and MOVEA drives the RISC OS pointer against the VIDC cursor registers --
see docs/lab/RISCOS3-WAVE.md for the five-target accuracy table.

Applies AFTER mame-ctlsock-ram-cursor.patch (it edits that patch's own new
lines) and touches nothing else.
